WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.320
If the price of salt doubled, you would still buy about the same. But if one brand of crisps got dearer, many people would switch to another brand.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:27.013
Price elasticity of demand, or P E D, measures how much quantity demanded responds to a price change. Divide the percentage change in quantity demanded by the percentage change in price. Price up ten per cent, quantity down twenty per cent: P E D is minus two.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:41.573
Ignore the minus sign and read the size. Zero is perfectly inelastic. Between zero and one, inelastic. Exactly one, unitary. Above one, elastic. Infinity, perfectly elastic.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:49.733
On a diagram, elastic demand is a flat curve. A small rise in price brings a big fall in quantity demanded.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:57.893
Inelastic demand is a steep curve. Even a big rise in price brings only a small fall in quantity demanded.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:10.691
Step one, define P E D. Step two, classify the value. Take minus one point five. Ignore the sign: one point five is above one, so demand is elastic.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:24.997
Step three, apply a reason to the product. Demand is more inelastic when there are few close substitutes, the good is a necessity or a habit, it takes a small share of income, or the time period is short.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:41.987
Step four, link to total revenue: price times quantity. Raise the price from two dollars to two dollars twenty. With inelastic demand, sales fall a little and revenue rises. With elastic demand, sales fall a lot and revenue falls.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:50.901
Firms use this to set prices. Governments use it too: taxing goods with inelastic demand raises more tax revenue.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:02:08.104
Here is a real question from November 2023. Marina Café, by the sea, charges two euros for a coffee. Other cafés sell the same brand for eighty cents. Analyse why P E D may be inelastic. Six marks.

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:16.040
Start with the meaning. Then say what inelastic means: quantity demanded changes by a smaller percentage than price.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:26.435
Reason one: few close substitutes. Other cafés sell the same coffee, but not by the sea with those views. So customers are unlikely to switch.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:35.597
Use the data. Customers already pay one euro twenty more than elsewhere. That is one hundred and fifty per cent more, and they still come.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:45.035
Reason two: a coffee is a small share of income. A small price rise hardly touches a tourist's budget, so they buy nearly as much.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:57.696
Conclude. A price rise brings a smaller percentage fall in sales, so the café could raise its total revenue. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:10.277
A common mistake is saying inelastic means people buy exactly the same. They still buy less, just by a smaller percentage. No change at all is perfectly inelastic.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:16.984
Another is getting the revenue rule backwards. With elastic demand, a price rise cuts revenue.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:33.395
Now you try. A town's only bus company raises fares by twenty per cent, and journeys fall by five per cent. Calculate P E D, classify it, give a reason, and state the revenue effect. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:47.341
P E D is minus five divided by twenty, which is minus nought point two five. Ignore the sign: nought point two five is between zero and one, so demand is inelastic.

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:57.008
There is only one bus company, so there is no other bus to switch to, and people keep travelling. The fare rise therefore increases total revenue.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:06.541
Remember: define P E D, classify the value, apply a reason to the case, and link it to revenue. Now try the practice questions.
